PRACTICAL NO. 17 CONTINUOUS INTEGRATION USING JENKINS

AIM: To perform Continuous Integration using Jenkins with Selenium
WebDriver.

TOOLS: Jenkins, Selenium WebDriver, Eclipse IDE, Java, TestNG.

  ------------------------------------------------------------
  STEP 1: CREATE JAVA PROJECT
  ------------------------------------------------------------
  1. Open Eclipse IDE. 2. Click File -> New -> Java Project.
  3. Enter Project Name: SeleniumTest 4. Click Finish.

  ------------------------------------------------------------

STEP 2: CREATE PACKAGE

1.  In Project Explorer, expand the project.
2.  Right-click on src.
3.  Select New -> Package.
4.  Enter Package Name: seleniumtest
5.  Click Finish.

  ------------------------------------------------------------
  STEP 3: ADD REQUIRED LIBRARIES
  ------------------------------------------------------------
  1. Right-click on the project. 2. Select Build Path ->
  Configure Build Path. 3. Open the Libraries tab. 4. Click
  Add External JARs. 5. Select the required Selenium and
  TestNG JAR files. 6. Click Apply and Close.

  ------------------------------------------------------------

STEP 4: CREATE JAVA CLASS

1.  Right-click on the seleniumtest package.
2.  Select New -> Class.
3.  Enter Class Name: GoogleTset
4.  Click Finish.
5.  Paste the following code and save the file.

FILE: GoogleTset.java

package seleniumtest;

import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver; import
org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver; import org.testng.Assert;
import org.testng.annotations.BeforeMethod; import
org.testng.annotations.Test;

public class GoogleTset {

    WebDriver driver;

    @BeforeMethod
    public void setUp() {
        driver = new ChromeDriver();
        driver.manage().window().maximize();
    }

    @Test
    public void googleTest() {

        driver.get("https://www.google.com");

        String title = driver.getTitle();

        System.out.println("Title: " + title);

        Assert.assertTrue(
            title.contains("Google"),
            "Title does not contain Google"
        );

        try {
            Thread.sleep(10000);
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }

}

  ------------------------------------------------------------
  STEP 5: CREATE testng.xml FILE
  ------------------------------------------------------------
  1. Right-click on the project in Eclipse. 2. Select New ->
  File. 3. Enter File Name: testng.xml 4. Click Finish. 5.
  Paste the following XML code. 6. Press Ctrl + S to save.

  FILE: testng.xml

  ------------------------------------------------------------

STEP 6: EXECUTE TESTNG XML

1.  Find testng.xml in Project Explorer.
2.  Right-click on testng.xml.
3.  Select Run As.
4.  Click TestNG Suite.
5.  Chrome browser should open.
6.  Google website should open.
7.  The program reads the page title and checks whether it contains
    “Google”.
8.  The test waits for 10 seconds and then finishes.
9.  Check the TestNG Results in Eclipse.

